{
  "dom_id": "step-q161045",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨((z-1)^{3}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{__________},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=(z-1)^{3}+1$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,2].$ What is the second component of $r?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14275690/assets/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)=(z-1)^{3}+1\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,2].$    \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨((z-1)^{3}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,((z-1)^{3}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [1,2],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $((z-1)^{3}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
